# Erdős Problem #99 — flat-pentagon contact-chain extraction

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** global pentagon face floor/degree-excess bookkeeping; empty-pentagon trapezoid stability; triangle-free contact-degree bound
**Claim class:** proof-only structural refinement awaiting independent audit
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The empty-pentagon endpoint theorem identifies the zero-residual pentagon with the flat
three-triangle lattice trapezoid. Its global extraction keeps pairwise vertex-disjoint
**five-vertex pentagons**, which costs a factor `1/21`. For the next deformation target
that is stronger than necessary. The local obstruction is already present in the
three-vertex boundary chain through the almost-flat middle vertex.

Let

\[
X:=Q-f,
\qquad
f_*:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over4}+0.00021
=0.167124643466509\ldots .
\]

On every Type-II subsequence with

\[
R_F=o(D),
\]

there is a scale `epsilon_D->0` for which the following two source-level inequalities
hold:

\[
\boxed{
X+5V_{flat}\ge(f_*-o(1))D,
}
\tag{FC.1}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
X+13C_{flat}\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
}
\tag{FC.2}
\]

Here:

- `V_flat` is the number of **distinct positive-core source vertices** that occur as
  the almost-flat middle vertex of one good pentagon and have two exact unit-contact
  neighbors whose angle at the middle vertex is at least `pi-epsilon_D`;
- `C_flat` is the maximum number of pairwise vertex-disjoint three-vertex contact
  chains `l-m-r` of that form.

Thus if `Q-f=o(D)`,

\[
\boxed{
{V_{flat}\over D}\ge {f_*\over5}-o(1)
=0.0334249286933018\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{FC.3}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
{C_{flat}\over D}\ge {f_*\over13}-o(1)
=0.0128557418051160\ldots-o(1).
}
\tag{FC.4}
\]

This is stronger for chain-based deformations than the `f_*/21` whole-pentagon
extraction. It also retains a continuous tradeoff with high-degree face excess.

## 1. Good flat pentagons versus degree excess

Let `p_5` be the number of bounded pentagonal exceptional faces. The exact degree
identity gives

\[
X=Q-f=\sum_{d\ge6}(d-5)n_d,
\]

so

\[
p_5\ge f-X.
\tag{FC.5}
\]

Fix `epsilon>0`. The empty-pentagon endpoint theorem and Wegner first-jump argument
supply a threshold `theta(epsilon)>0` such that every pentagon with

\[
r(P)<\theta(\epsilon)
\]

has no assigned interior isolate and, after labeling its boundary as
`l,m,r,a,b`, satisfies

\[
|l-m|=|m-r|=1,
\qquad
\angle lmr>\pi-\epsilon,
\tag{FC.6}
\]

and is `epsilon`-close to the flat unit trapezoid.

On `R_F=o(D)`, all local residuals are nonnegative and sum to `R_F`, so the number of
pentagons failing this fixed threshold is `o(D)`. If `G_epsilon` is the number of good
pentagons, then

\[
G_\epsilon\ge p_5-o(D).
\]

Combining with (FC.5) and the AG42/Section-171 face floor

\[
f\ge(f_*-o(1))D
\]

gives

\[
\boxed{
X+G_\epsilon\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
}
\tag{FC.7}
\]

This relation is valid without assuming `X=o(D)`.

## 2. Distinct almost-flat core vertices: loss only `1/5`

Choose the distinguished almost-flat middle vertex `m(P)` from each good pentagon.
For sufficiently small fixed `epsilon`, the trapezoid stability neighborhood makes
this middle vertex unambiguous; alternatively choose any vertex satisfying (FC.6).

A triangle-free unit-contact graph has contact degree at most five. Around a source
vertex, each bounded face containing that vertex occupies an incident face sector, so
one source vertex belongs to at most five bounded exceptional faces. Consequently one
source vertex can be selected as `m(P)` for at most five good pentagons.

If `V_flat(epsilon)` is the number of distinct selected middle vertices, then

\[
G_\epsilon\le5V_{flat}(\epsilon).
\tag{FC.8}
\]

Together with (FC.7),

\[
\boxed{
X+5V_{flat}(\epsilon)
\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
}
\tag{FC.9}
\]

In particular

\[
\max\left\{{X\over D},{V_{flat}(\epsilon)\over D}\right\}
\ge {f_*\over6}-o(1)
=0.0278541072444181\ldots-o(1).
\tag{FC.10}
\]

## 3. Vertex-disjoint near-straight unit-contact chains: loss `1/13`

For each good pentagon retain only its three-vertex path

\[
l(P)-m(P)-r(P).
\]

Build the conflict graph whose vertices are these paths and where two paths are
adjacent when they share a source vertex. A fixed source vertex lies on at most five
bounded exceptional faces, and only one path was selected from each face. Therefore a
source vertex belongs to at most five selected paths.

Each selected path has three source vertices. Through each of them there are at most
four other paths, so the conflict graph has maximum degree at most

\[
3(5-1)=12.
\tag{FC.11}
\]

A greedy independent set therefore keeps at least `1/13` of all good paths. If
`C_flat(epsilon)` denotes the maximum vertex-disjoint subfamily,

\[
C_{flat}(\epsilon)\ge {G_\epsilon\over13}.
\tag{FC.12}
\]

Using (FC.7),

\[
\boxed{
X+13C_{flat}(\epsilon)
\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
}
\tag{FC.13}
\]

The corresponding unweighted alternative is

\[
\max\left\{{X\over D},{C_{flat}(\epsilon)\over D}\right\}
\ge {f_*\over14}-o(1)
=0.0119374745333220\ldots-o(1).
\tag{FC.14}
\]

## 4. Vanishing-turn diagonalization

For every fixed `epsilon>0`, (FC.9) and (FC.13) hold with only `o(D)` loss on an
`R_F=o(D)` subsequence. Apply them successively to `epsilon_j=1/j`. Because
`R_F/D->0`, choose a nondecreasing index `j(D)->infinity` slowly enough that the
high-residual exceptional loss at threshold `theta(epsilon_{j(D)})` remains `o(D)`.
Set

\[
\epsilon_D:=\epsilon_{j(D)}\to0.
\]

Then (FC.1)--(FC.2) hold at this vanishing turn scale. In the low-degree-excess corner
`X=o(D)`, the extracted chains satisfy

\[
|l-m|=|m-r|=1,
\qquad
\angle lmr=\pi-o(1),
\]

with the coefficient in (FC.4).

## 5. Frontier effect

The hard small-residual, low-degree-excess branch can now be stated without carrying
whole pentagonal holes:

```text
Omega(D) distinct positive-core vertices
with two exact unit contacts becoming antipodal,
and Omega(D) vertex-disjoint length-two unit-contact chains
with total turning angle o(1) per chain.
```

The next useful theorem can therefore attack a three-vertex source object directly.
Possible mechanisms are a quantitative KKT force-balance constraint at the middle
vertex, a source-preserving straight-chain flex, or a charge of these almost-zero turns
to the diameter bracing system. None of those consequences is asserted here.

## 6. Scope audit

- The theorem is conditional on the true force-hull residual branch `R_F=o(D)`.
- No assumption `Q-f=o(D)` is needed for the tradeoff forms (FC.1)--(FC.2).
- `V_flat` counts distinct actual source vertices in the positive contact core.
- `C_flat` counts pairwise vertex-disjoint actual three-source contact paths; it does
  not count whole pentagons.
- The factor `1/13` uses only the canonical triangle-free contact-degree bound and one
  chosen path per bounded face.
- The flattening input is exactly the already-classified empty-pentagon trapezoid
  endpoint; no new equality classification is imported.
- No exact hidden contact, unit equilateral triangle, or strict sub-`D` competitor is
  produced.

C-II and Level I remain open.
